NEW_SECTION
Six commented blocks: hero, playground (with a rebuild function and a generated
equivalent-code panel), the consumer-handle split view, an edgeResizer() helper
instantiated four times, the teardown panel, and a table of thirteen throwing cases.

REASON
The file is written as documentation, per CLAUDE.md, so the comments carry the sharp
edges rather than restating the calls.

The playground is the structural decision worth recording. config is read once, by
initialize(), and there is no live reconfiguration — so a control panel is only
possible by destroying the instance and building a new one on every change. That is
why buildPlayResizer() opens with `if (playResizer != null) playResizer.destroy()`,
and it is the clearest demonstration of why destroy() had to be added: without it
each control change would stack another live Resizer, with its own listeners, on the
same element.

Controls are bound on "change" rather than "input" for the same reason. A slider or a
colour picker firing on "input" would tear down and rebuild the instance on every
pixel of travel. The numeric readouts beside the sliders are bound separately on
"input" so they still track continuously.

The idle-status timer is parked on the instance the callback is handed (r._idle)
rather than on the outer playResizer variable, which is reassigned on every rebuild.

Each validation case constructs a throwaway Resizer over a real element rather than
reusing one of the page's working instances, so a rejected setter cannot leave a live
component half-configured.

edgeResizer() takes the axis and the edge and varies nothing else, which keeps the
four instances in section 03 obviously identical apart from the one property the
section is about.
